## Run locally

```bash
{{if eq .Database.Engine "postgresql"}}export DATABASE_URL='jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/{{.ProjectName}}'
export DATABASE_USER='postgres'
export DATABASE_PASSWORD='replace-me'
{{else}}export DATABASE_URL='jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/{{.ProjectName}}?useSSL=true&requireSSL=true'
export DATABASE_USER='root'
export DATABASE_PASSWORD='replace-me'
{{end}}mvn spring-boot:run
```

Set `BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_EMAIL` and `BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD` together on the first
run. The password must contain at least 12 characters. Remove both bootstrap
variables after the account exists. `COOKIE_SECURE` defaults to `true`; set it to
`false` only for local HTTP development.

The service exposes `GET /healthz` for process liveness and `GET /readyz` for a
bounded database readiness check. Browser sessions use an HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax
cookie, while automation uses `Authorization: Bearer <token>`. Raw session and API
tokens are returned only to their owners and only SHA-256 digests are persisted.
Database errors are never returned to clients. The HTTP contract is
`api/openapi.yaml`.
{{if .Jobs}}

## Background worker

The durable queue uses idempotent enqueue, bounded payloads, expiring leases,
heartbeat renewal, exponential retry, and a terminal dead state. Start an independent
worker process after building the service:

```bash
java -jar target/{{.ArtifactID}}-0.1.0-SNAPSHOT.jar \
  --spring.main.web-application-type=none --scaffold.worker.enabled=true
```

Set `WORKER_ID` to a stable unique process name when desired. Add Spring beans that
implement `JobWorker.Handler`; handlers must honor thread interruption and must not
log payload contents. The web process leaves the worker disabled by default.
{{end}}
{{if .Notifications}}

## Email notifications

Feature code calls `NotificationService.enqueueEmail` with a stable idempotency key;
there is deliberately no generic email HTTP endpoint. The worker requires the
following runtime-only configuration:

```bash
export SMTP_ADDRESS='smtp.example.com:587'
export SMTP_FROM='Example <app@example.com>'
export SMTP_TLS_MODE='starttls' # starttls or tls
export SMTP_USERNAME='mailer'  # optional only as a pair
export SMTP_PASSWORD='replace-me'
```

TLS 1.2 or newer, certificate hostname verification, and bounded connection/read/write
timeouts are mandatory. Queued email bodies remain sensitive database payloads until
your retention policy removes them; never log payloads or place SMTP credentials in
source, Blueprints, or AI context.
{{end}}
{{if .Files}}

## File assets

Set `FILE_STORAGE_ROOT` to a shared durable volume before starting the service.
Authenticated callers can upload, list, inspect, download, and delete assets through
`/api/v1/files`. Uploads are spooled by the servlet container and streamed into a
same-directory temporary object with a hard 10 MiB limit and SHA-256 digest. The
local adapter publishes with a create-only hard link, rejects unsafe storage keys and
symbolic-link directories, and compensates metadata/blob mutation failures.

Only public metadata crosses the HTTP boundary; the generated storage key is never
serialized. A production multi-instance deployment must mount the same durable volume
or replace `BlobStore` with an adapter that preserves the same create-only, bounded,
streaming, and compensation contract.
{{end}}
{{if .Cache}}

## Application cache

`CacheService` stores replica-coherent JSON in {{.Database.DisplayName}} with a
tenant or global scope, a 191-byte key bound, a 256 KiB value bound, and a maximum
30-day TTL. Missing, expired, and cross-tenant keys return the same miss result.
Deletes are idempotent, and `purgeExpired` accepts at most 10,000 rows per call.

Namespace keys by feature, choose a stable TTL, and invalidate on authoritative
mutations. Do not cache secrets or expose a generic cache HTTP endpoint. A Redis
repository may replace the JDBC adapter only if it preserves the same scope, size,
expiry, miss, and bounded-cleanup semantics.
{{end}}
{{if .JobAdmin}}

## Background job administration

Administrators with `jobs:read` can inspect bounded metadata pages at
`GET /api/v1/jobs`; `jobs:manage` permits only `POST /api/v1/jobs/{id}/retry` for
dead jobs. The response contract never includes payloads, scope keys, or
deduplication keys. A retry locks the scoped row, resets attempts and lease state,
returns it to `queued`, and writes its audit event in the same transaction.

Do not add payload inspection or editing, running-job takeover, unbounded listing,
cross-tenant lookup, or direct queue-table updates to operational tooling.
{{end}}
{{if .Observability}}

## HTTP observability

Every HTTP request receives a validated or generated `X-Request-ID`. Access logs
contain only that identifier, method, bounded URL path, response status, and
duration; query strings, headers, bodies, and exception values are excluded.
`GET /metrics` exposes only aggregate process counters without user-derived labels.

`GET /readyz` runs the whole database probe behind a two-second deadline and returns
only `ready` or `unavailable`; `GET /healthz` remains independent of the database.
Protect operational endpoints at the ingress or private-network boundary. Metrics
reset when the process restarts and are not aggregated across replicas.
{{end}}
{{if .CSVTransfer}}

## CSV import and export

The administration API exposes `{{.Business.RoutePath}}/import`,
`{{.Business.RoutePath}}/import-template`, and `{{.Business.RoutePath}}/export`
with separate `:import` and `:export` permissions. CSV columns always follow the
Blueprint field order. Each document is limited to 5 MiB and 1,000 data rows.

Import is create-only: the complete document is parsed before persistence, and all
rows plus one audit event commit in one transaction. Export uses one bounded,
organization-scoped query and emits no partial document. Text cells that could be
spreadsheet formulas use a reversible apostrophe escape. Use a background-job and
object-storage workflow for larger migrations; do not relax these synchronous limits.
{{end}}
{{if .Approvals}}

## Approval workflows

The approval API provides separate submitter permissions (`approvals:submit` and
`approvals:mine`) and reviewer permissions (`approvals:read` and
`approvals:decide`). Submission verifies the generated business subject in the
current organization and permits only one pending request for that subject.

Requesters cannot approve or reject their own requests, and only the requester may
cancel a pending request. Rejection requires a reason. Every terminal operation
must send the current decimal-string `version`; each successful submission or
transition commits the request, one immutable event, and one audit event in the
same transaction. A decision records evidence only and never mutates the business
subject implicitly.
{{end}}
{{if .Catalog}}

## Commerce catalog

The catalog capability generates a product domain independently from Blueprint
CRUD modules. Administration routes under `/api/v1/catalog/products` require
separate read and write permissions. Anonymous routes under
`/api/v1/storefront/products` return active products only and hide products owned
by inactive organizations.

SKU and currency values normalize to upper case. Prices use the currency's
smallest unit and cross JSON as decimal strings with optimistic versions. Products
move only from `draft` to `active` to terminal `archived`; every create, update,
publish, or archive operation commits its audit event in the same transaction.
Tenant-enabled storefront requests require `X-Organization-ID`; the generated
Nuxt proxy reads it from server-only `SCAFFOLD_ORGANIZATION_ID`.
{{end}}
{{if .Tenancy}}

Authenticated users create and discover organizations through
`POST /api/v1/organizations` and `GET /api/v1/organizations`. Every business
request must send the selected organization in `X-Organization-ID`. Authorization
uses that user's membership role, and every business query and mutation includes
the same organization predicate; never bypass the generated tenant service or
repository methods.
{{end}}
{{if .TenancyMembers}}

Organization administrators can list members, create 72-hour email-bound
invitations, change member roles, and remove access. Invitation tokens are returned
only once; persistence stores only a SHA-256 digest. Invitation acceptance requires
an authenticated identity with the invited email. Keep member mutations inside the
generated repository transaction so concurrent changes cannot remove the final
administrator.
{{end}}
{{if .TenancyLifecycle}}

The organization creator is its initial owner. Active administrators may rename
the organization; only the owner may transfer ownership, deactivate, or reactivate
it. Transfer targets an existing member and promotes that identity to administrator.
Deactivation retains all tenant data but blocks new tenant-authorized requests.
Transfer ownership before changing or removing the owner; do not add a cascading
organization deletion shortcut.
{{end}}
